Using $40.5$ femtobarns of data collected by the LHCb experiment at 13 TeV, this study aims to determine the LHCb experiment's sensitivity in identifying the sign of the $c$-quark mass term within the differential decay rate of the process $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$. By employing various approaches and assuming that the underlying process is described by the standard model, researchers have determined the sensitivity levels for different assumptions regarding the degree of precision in calculating the differential decay rate and the method used to analyze the data. These sensitivity metrics range from $0.13$ to $0.51$ times $10^{-4}$ when calculated using a $3\sigma$ confidence interval and the most precise method available to analyze the data. Furthermore, this study facilitates the evaluation of specific form factors that describe the hadronic process $B_sightarrow D_s^{*-}$. Specifically, it assesses the uncertainty associated with the calculated decay rate, which is directly related to the degree of precision in calculating the relevant form factors and determining the sign of the $c$-quark mass term within the differential decay rate of the process $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$. A comprehensive analysis indicates that in the standard model, the uncertainty associated with the calculated decay rate is as little as $0.13$ times $10^{-4}$, which is primarily influenced by the degree of precision in calculating the form factors and assumes that the data is analyzed using the most precise method available. Conversely, the same analysis shows that in the standard model, the sign of the $c$-quark mass term within the differential decay rate of the process $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
